#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12845067-a8b7-5913-979e-1299091f1d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570159} {:answer/id #uuid "16f6109b-7508-5135-8923-7bab55919240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570170} {:answer/id #uuid "c55f1198-aad6-521b-bae5-b64244ff77e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570468} {:answer/id #uuid "cef3c9ba-eb28-5a61-91c0-f5a03b59dee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570483} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4b47cc5-e313-59aa-949c-897284af5182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570558} :answer-field/id #uuid "15617f41-35a9-548a-b217-4bc5608003fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152269" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=21-4x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}-{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2} \\\\ y &=21-4x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =21-4x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-21 & =0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-7,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,49).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,9).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=49,\\,{y}_{2}=9,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}=49-9=40.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca9ba76d66d80a0a564fbefd02342d3f63d99f6fbd907b736cdc9acee79b174.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40949b7c-a27c-5611-b404-6149a0b8d050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570233} {:answer/id #uuid "4d93872e-5c7a-54f9-bdf4-ce3a1167345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570262} {:answer/id #uuid "adcb1ac8-48fc-566e-a31f-2be7b6ee6dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570434} {:answer/id #uuid "b4792ac3-9031-5945-8164-12455d78b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570449} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d37f8a21-89a6-581f-bd81-65529db39d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570496} :answer-field/id #uuid "978d262c-3412-5d34-a3c8-3416c0e3c873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152280" :question/problem "The parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and the line $y=2x+1$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=3,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1+3=4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7751473a1f9c40086a3a765487cf24f8e5430150d015cd64f1ed0c5bd62fcee4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f0c16c-499b-5a14-9e19-173e4d918a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570175} {:answer/id #uuid "1db6d43d-85f2-5fa4-ae72-336840f36edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570180} {:answer/id #uuid "5594fa57-8458-511c-9aeb-7fe140e03b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570275} {:answer/id #uuid "691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570310} {:answer/id #uuid "abc497cf-9291-5541-a458-1cc9c303bee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "691119d4-06b9-55bd-8f14-b3a8e2f8e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570310} :answer-field/id #uuid "91cdd488-37fe-52d7-879d-76d1056cf280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155535" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+3x-1$ and the line $y=-3x-10$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+3x-1 \\\\ y &=-3x-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =-3x-10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-1+3x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-10 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(-3,-1).$ Therefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-1,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/91/91bedb41cd1b30cf392b23c0986e7b191ecf170fbdd4a0c34f5e2b8977c747b2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31701e1b-752e-53b5-87f8-c950c55efe89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570209} {:answer/id #uuid "79ab7361-c55a-518b-a40b-db0377da228a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570340} {:answer/id #uuid "af5a6d7b-5c27-5373-b0c3-f3df6c003fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570441} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8b0a8-1117-56ef-a3e6-79add5f0af91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570459} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3e5360-5da9-5043-989a-9ac296e89f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7734cd7d-32e7-59ba-99af-993307dacc38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" :question/math-academy-id "q-155959" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+2k$ intersects the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}-x \\\\ y &=x+2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & =x+2k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=2,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4+16k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+16k & <0 \\\\ 16k & <-4 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ k & <-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3399484a-1e9d-5f6e-9670-2f57b635951b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3399484a-1e9d-5f6e-9670-2f57b635951b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570216} :answer-field/id #uuid "21fc663a-42dd-5170-baa9-33270847b513" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaa25441-f037-5889-b8ee-98ed9ac7af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570426} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb279d1-a081-5445-83f9-b74769bc07da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-250171" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+3k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+3k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y &=x+3k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =x+3k \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(1)(-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-16 \\\\ k & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-2.$"}]}
